  "rationale": "Both answers and the English sense are correct, but the card omits a material shared-synonym contrast: Indonesian also uses “majikan.” Presenting the pair as simply different common words may lead learners to infer that “majikan” is not Indonesian. Malaysian “majikan” is directly supported as employer; Indonesian “pemberi kerja” is the broad, neutral term, while “majikan” is also valid and can carry boss/household-employer nuances.",
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "subject": "Indonesian answer: pemberi kerja",
      "conclusion": "Correct and standard for the broad sense “employer,” including individuals, companies, and other employing entities.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://jdih.kemenkeu.go.id/kamus-hukum/pemberi-kerja?id=673d723e81190833df313c8eb3c03592"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Malaysian answer: majikan",
      "conclusion": "Correct and established Malaysian Malay term for a person or organization that employs or gives work to others.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=202792&keyword=majikan"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "English cue and sense",
      "conclusion": "The cue “employer” and sense “person or organization that employs a worker” align with both entries.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://jdih.kemenkeu.go.id/kamus-hukum/pemberi-kerja?id=673d723e81190833df313c8eb3c03592",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=202792&keyword=majikan"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Final relation: different-common-word",
      "conclusion": "Acceptable only as a preference contrast, not as an exclusive language distinction: Indonesian also uses “majikan,” so the relation needs qualification.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.web.id/majikan",
        "https://jdih.kemenkeu.go.id/kamus-hukum/pemberi-kerja?id=673d723e81190833df313c8eb3c03592"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "False-friend contrast",
      "conclusion": "No genuine false-friend contrast is present. The missing note should state that “majikan” is also Indonesian, often with boss or household-employer nuance, while “pemberi kerja” is broader and more neutral.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
